00:08:44.822 --> 00:08:46.530
Or are we just in the
streaming business?

00:08:46.530 --> 00:08:47.790
AMIT PANDE: No, absolutely.

00:08:47.790 --> 00:08:51.060
In fact, when we think about
how machine learning can

00:08:51.060 --> 00:08:53.850
help transform your media
and entertainment business,

00:08:53.850 --> 00:08:55.980
we think about three buckets.

00:08:55.980 --> 00:08:58.560
We think machine learning can
help you innovate and build

00:08:58.560 --> 00:09:00.710
those next generation
experiences.

00:09:00.710 --> 00:09:03.690
It can help you better
monetize your content.

00:09:03.690 --> 00:09:06.690
And it can help you reduce
your operational cost,

00:09:06.690 --> 00:09:09.120
while delivering your
content at global scale.

00:09:09.120 --> 00:09:10.567
FRANCESC CAMPOY: How?

00:09:10.567 --> 00:09:12.650
MARK MANDEL: I was going
to say, you say innovate.

00:09:12.650 --> 00:09:14.010
What does that mean?

00:09:14.010 --> 00:09:15.180
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Yeah.

00:09:15.180 --> 00:09:15.888
AMIT PANDE: Yeah.

00:09:15.888 --> 00:09:19.020
So let's take a
typical supply chain

00:09:19.020 --> 00:09:21.210
for content business
[INAUDIBLE]..

00:09:21.210 --> 00:09:24.420
Let's take the creative
process of content creation.

00:09:24.420 --> 00:09:29.040
We can apply machine learning to
help you determine exact clips

00:09:29.040 --> 00:09:31.380
and automatically
generate highlights

00:09:31.380 --> 00:09:34.120
for your content using
machine learning.

00:09:34.120 --> 00:09:35.280
Let's take another example.

00:09:35.280 --> 00:09:38.760
Content recommendation has been
around for a very long time.

00:09:38.760 --> 00:09:40.680
And traditionally,
content recommendation

00:09:40.680 --> 00:09:42.940
was based on content metadata.

00:09:42.940 --> 00:09:46.050
We can use machine learning
to actually understand

00:09:46.050 --> 00:09:48.060
what's happening
inside your video

00:09:48.060 --> 00:09:51.092
and help you find
visually similar videos

00:09:51.092 --> 00:09:52.050
that you can recommend.

00:09:52.050 --> 00:09:55.260
So it can be another signal
that can feed into your content

00:09:55.260 --> 00:09:56.460
recommendation system.

00:09:56.460 --> 00:09:58.420
Another example is
machine learning

00:09:58.420 --> 00:10:01.530
can automatically
microsegment your content

00:10:01.530 --> 00:10:04.870
and analyze and draw
insights each of the segments

00:10:04.870 --> 00:10:05.930
of your content.

00:10:05.930 --> 00:10:08.350
If you had a TV
show and you wanted

00:10:08.350 --> 00:10:11.710
to understand which actors
are drawing more audience

00:10:11.710 --> 00:10:14.730
or which scenes are
seeing higher engagement,

00:10:14.730 --> 00:10:17.320
with machine learning
now you have the ability

00:10:17.320 --> 00:10:21.910
to both combine your audience
retention data and the deeper

00:10:21.910 --> 00:10:26.080
segmentation of your content
to draw these kind of insights.

00:10:26.080 --> 00:10:28.210
FRANCESC CAMPOY: This
made me think about that--

00:10:28.210 --> 00:10:29.440
I think it was IBM.

00:10:29.440 --> 00:10:33.820
They did this research where
they created a movie trailer

00:10:33.820 --> 00:10:36.144
from movie directly using AI.

00:10:36.144 --> 00:10:38.060
Is this the kind of thing
we're talking about?

00:10:38.060 --> 00:10:39.268
AMIT PANDE: Yeah, absolutely.

00:10:39.268 --> 00:10:41.830
All the way from being
able to create trailers

00:10:41.830 --> 00:10:44.860
to being able to create
automatic highlights

00:10:44.860 --> 00:10:45.820
or previous, correct?

00:10:45.820 --> 00:10:48.460
So let's take an example
for you're probably

00:10:48.460 --> 00:10:50.495
familiar with the "Chopped"
cooking game show.

00:10:50.495 --> 00:10:51.370
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Yes.

00:10:51.370 --> 00:10:53.770
AMIT PANDE: And [INAUDIBLE]
can combine all of the signals,

00:10:53.770 --> 00:10:54.270
correct?

00:10:54.270 --> 00:10:57.020
Like it can automatically
segment your video.

00:10:57.020 --> 00:10:59.890
It can annotate each of the
segments inside your video,

00:10:59.890 --> 00:11:04.270
rank the segments based on
your audience engagement data.

00:11:04.270 --> 00:11:07.180
And it can help identify things
like, where is the start,

00:11:07.180 --> 00:11:08.900
where is the middle and end?

00:11:08.900 --> 00:11:10.900
And not only that,
it can also start

00:11:10.900 --> 00:11:13.327
looking at background,
audio, to understand

00:11:13.327 --> 00:11:15.910
where those interesting moments
are happening inside your game

00:11:15.910 --> 00:11:16.660
show.

00:11:16.660 --> 00:11:19.780
And then using all of this--
like as a user, if you come in

00:11:19.780 --> 00:11:21.760
and it's like, I only
have three minutes.

00:11:21.760 --> 00:11:23.590
And I want a quick highlight.

00:11:23.590 --> 00:11:25.900
We can generate that
highlight on demand, correct?

00:11:25.900 --> 00:11:28.930
So it's almost helping
you consume your content

00:11:28.930 --> 00:11:31.186
on demand based on
your requirements

00:11:31.186 --> 00:11:32.560
Similarly for the
games, correct?

00:11:32.560 --> 00:11:34.120
Like, you might be--

00:11:34.120 --> 00:11:35.230
there's a game yesterday.

00:11:35.230 --> 00:11:37.630
And I want to see a highlight
for yesterday's game

00:11:37.630 --> 00:11:39.190
for this particular
player, correct?

00:11:39.190 --> 00:11:40.520
MARK MANDEL: So I'm curious.

00:11:40.520 --> 00:11:41.710
So the stuff you're
kind of talking about

00:11:41.710 --> 00:11:43.751
