00:14:47.381 --> 00:14:49.630
MARK MANDEL: So I'll ask an
interesting question, too.

00:14:49.630 --> 00:14:51.730
So I mean, this all
sounds super, super cool

00:14:51.730 --> 00:14:53.230
and crazy and wonderful.

00:14:53.230 --> 00:14:55.210
Does this mean that
using this technology,

00:14:55.210 --> 00:14:57.580
like we're removing humans
from the application?

00:14:57.580 --> 00:14:59.740
Or like is this replacing that?

00:14:59.740 --> 00:15:02.350
Or is this a tool that people
can use in conjunction?

00:15:02.350 --> 00:15:05.255
Or how do you see this fitting
in the wider spectrum there?

00:15:05.255 --> 00:15:06.880
AMIT PANDE: I think
from my perspective

00:15:06.880 --> 00:15:09.213
when I think about machine
learning, a very good example

00:15:09.213 --> 00:15:10.610
is self-driving cars, correct?

00:15:10.610 --> 00:15:13.400
Like we want to get to that
autonomous driving cars.

00:15:13.400 --> 00:15:16.190
And we are probably five
years out from that.

00:15:16.190 --> 00:15:18.110
But meanwhile, we are
building out these steps

00:15:18.110 --> 00:15:21.410
towards having a fully
autonomous car which

00:15:21.410 --> 00:15:23.330
are, today, assistive steps.

00:15:23.330 --> 00:15:25.610
So similarly, when I think
of about machine learning,

00:15:25.610 --> 00:15:27.800
it's starting in
that assistive step.

00:15:27.800 --> 00:15:30.380
And then gradually, it's
going to learn and learn,

00:15:30.380 --> 00:15:31.460
so that it gets better.

00:15:31.460 --> 00:15:33.260
And probably at
some stage, it'll

00:15:33.260 --> 00:15:37.590
fully automate the steps
that it's being applied to.

00:15:37.590 --> 00:15:41.830
I also feel over time the
way we do our processes,

00:15:41.830 --> 00:15:42.800
they will change.

00:15:42.800 --> 00:15:44.420
And you'll probably
be able to do it

00:15:44.420 --> 00:15:46.400
more efficiently at lower cost.

00:15:46.400 --> 00:15:48.530
You'll probably be able
to delight your customers

00:15:48.530 --> 00:15:50.000
with better experiences.

00:15:50.000 --> 00:15:52.040
And the roles of
humans will change

00:15:52.040 --> 00:15:56.750
from doing repetitive tasks
to actually analyzing the data

00:15:56.750 --> 00:15:59.177
and using the machines
more intelligently to be

00:15:59.177 --> 00:16:00.260
able to perform the tasks.

00:16:00.260 --> 00:16:01.100
MARK MANDEL: So
if you're looking

00:16:01.100 --> 00:16:02.480
at something like
speech to text,

00:16:02.480 --> 00:16:03.855
like doing closed
captioning, you

00:16:03.855 --> 00:16:05.750
might use something
like speech to text API.

00:16:05.750 --> 00:16:07.970
But then the human might be
just verifying and making sure

00:16:07.970 --> 00:16:09.240
that's all correct,
something like that.

00:16:09.240 --> 00:16:09.830
AMIT PANDE: Yeah.

00:16:09.830 --> 00:16:11.246
That's actually a
very good point.

00:16:11.246 --> 00:16:14.780
So I believe because where
the technology is right now,

00:16:14.780 --> 00:16:17.870
a lot of these technologies
will benefit when you combine

00:16:17.870 --> 00:16:19.910
machine learning with humans.

00:16:19.910 --> 00:16:21.890
So if you take the
output of speech to text

00:16:21.890 --> 00:16:25.190
and combine it with an edit
tool where you can both quickly

00:16:25.190 --> 00:16:28.400
review and edit and
any changes you make,

00:16:28.400 --> 00:16:31.650
those actually feed back into
the machine learning model.

00:16:31.650 --> 00:16:34.130
It will significantly
improve the precision

00:16:34.130 --> 00:16:37.590
and recall that you see for
a future result of that API.

00:16:37.590 --> 00:16:41.540
So I think that's one of the
key areas I want to highlight.

00:16:41.540 --> 00:16:43.820
As you're thinking about
using machine learning,

00:16:43.820 --> 00:16:46.850
always think about how
can I train the models

00:16:46.850 --> 00:16:48.800
on context specific data.

00:16:48.800 --> 00:16:51.770
And how can I use a
feedback mechanism

00:16:51.770 --> 00:16:54.080
to further improve the
precision and recall

00:16:54.080 --> 00:16:57.230
of the machine learning APIs
or the results of my machine

00:16:57.230 --> 00:16:58.001
learning model?

00:16:58.001 --> 00:16:59.750
MARK MANDEL: Are their
feedback mechanisms

00:16:59.750 --> 00:17:02.881
for something like text to
speech or Cloud Vision API?

00:17:02.881 --> 00:17:05.339
AMIT PANDE: So right now, the
public APIs don't allow that.

00:17:05.339 --> 00:17:08.599
But you can imagine
building your own custom

00:17:08.599 --> 00:17:11.180
model that further allows
you to feed that back.

00:17:11.180 --> 00:17:14.630
And we are looking at
options for enabling, so

00:17:14.630 --> 00:17:16.910
that we can continue to help
you improve your quality.

00:17:16.910 --> 00:17:19.730
FRANCESC CAMPOY: I'm thinking
that we should try, as a demo,

00:17:19.730 --> 00:17:21.172
to do it on our own transcripts.

00:17:21.172 --> 00:17:22.130
MARK MANDEL: We should.

00:17:22.130 --> 00:17:23.210
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Yeah,
that would be fun.

00:17:23.210 --> 00:17:24.200
MARK MANDEL: So we
could actually do it.

00:17:24.200 --> 00:17:27.011
So we automate our transcripts
