00:17:42.710 --> 00:17:43.310
correct?

00:17:43.310 --> 00:17:45.290
So all that data
we're just training

00:17:45.290 --> 00:17:48.600
is going to help power the
APIs that you're using.

00:17:48.600 --> 00:17:50.600
FRANCESC CAMPOY: So I
publish a bunch of videos.

00:17:50.600 --> 00:17:52.340
I have a YouTube channel.

00:17:52.340 --> 00:17:54.650
And one of the coolest
things of posting on YouTube

00:17:54.650 --> 00:17:57.080
is that you get the closed
caption, and then also

00:17:57.080 --> 00:17:59.547
the automatic translations
to a bunch of languages--

00:17:59.547 --> 00:18:00.380
AMIT PANDE: Exactly.

00:18:00.380 --> 00:18:01.620
FRANCESC CAMPOY: --which
is very useful for people

00:18:01.620 --> 00:18:03.410
that do not understand English.

00:18:03.410 --> 00:18:06.570
Is that something that we can
do real time on a live stream,

00:18:06.570 --> 00:18:07.130
for instance?

00:18:07.130 --> 00:18:09.495
Or are we still not there?

00:18:09.495 --> 00:18:11.870
AMIT PANDE: I think right now,
we're obviously not there.

00:18:11.870 --> 00:18:15.140
But I do foresee that in
the next 6 to 12 months,

00:18:15.140 --> 00:18:16.850
the technology
will get to a point

00:18:16.850 --> 00:18:20.100
where we will start
applying this to real time.

00:18:20.100 --> 00:18:22.040
There are some tricks
available where

00:18:22.040 --> 00:18:24.482
you can pre-train your
models on context specific.

00:18:24.482 --> 00:18:26.690
Like, for instance, if you
have a sporting game which

00:18:26.690 --> 00:18:29.270
is where you probably care
about being real time,

00:18:29.270 --> 00:18:33.782
you can train the model
specifically for that context.

00:18:33.782 --> 00:18:35.240
And then the real
time output would

00:18:35.240 --> 00:18:37.457
be pretty good in terms of
quality here at the level

00:18:37.457 --> 00:18:40.040
that that is acceptable from a
closed captioning [INAUDIBLE]..

00:18:40.040 --> 00:18:41.831
So there are some tricks
available to being

00:18:41.831 --> 00:18:44.090
able to apply
almost in real time

00:18:44.090 --> 00:18:46.340
and getting the results
to the quality level

00:18:46.340 --> 00:18:48.020
that that matches
your expectation.

00:18:48.020 --> 00:18:48.600
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Cool.

00:18:48.600 --> 00:18:50.016
So we've been
talking a little bit

00:18:50.016 --> 00:18:52.850
about how to make the
broadcasting experience

00:18:52.850 --> 00:18:55.280
and the process itself
more pleasant, I'd say,

00:18:55.280 --> 00:18:57.750
or more automated,
better experience.

00:18:57.750 --> 00:19:00.110
But you also mention
monetization, money.

00:19:00.110 --> 00:19:00.950
I like that.

00:19:00.950 --> 00:19:04.260
How do you apply machine
learning for profit?

00:19:04.260 --> 00:19:05.810
How do you make
money out of this?

00:19:05.810 --> 00:19:06.250
AMIT PANDE: Yeah.

00:19:06.250 --> 00:19:08.083
So one of the things
machine learning can do

00:19:08.083 --> 00:19:10.250
is it can understand your video.

00:19:10.250 --> 00:19:12.680
And it can help you find
those specific moments

00:19:12.680 --> 00:19:15.150
in the video where there
are commercial intents.

00:19:15.150 --> 00:19:19.730
So it can annotate it with
products and labels and brands

00:19:19.730 --> 00:19:23.540
that the users are talking
about in your video itself.

00:19:23.540 --> 00:19:26.830
And it can also help you figure
out exactly how many ads should

00:19:26.830 --> 00:19:27.330
you show.

00:19:27.330 --> 00:19:30.380
So today, in most cases, if
you're watching a content,

00:19:30.380 --> 00:19:32.570
you probably have a
pre-determined number of ads,

00:19:32.570 --> 00:19:35.570
like maybe three ads or four
ads that you've come up with.

00:19:35.570 --> 00:19:39.050
And generally, that is
a client-side logic.

00:19:39.050 --> 00:19:41.540
We can combine that
with service-side logic

00:19:41.540 --> 00:19:45.320
and a learning algorithm which
says for this particular user,

00:19:45.320 --> 00:19:48.490
if I show a fourth ad,
he will likely drop off.

00:19:48.490 --> 00:19:51.680
But for this, another
user maybe showing two ads

00:19:51.680 --> 00:19:53.600
is that optimal number.

00:19:53.600 --> 00:19:55.750
And the duration of those
ads as well, correct?

00:19:55.750 --> 00:19:59.380
Like we've already starting
to see the 30 second ads.

00:19:59.380 --> 00:20:02.690
There's huge buzz in the
industry on bumper ads,

00:20:02.690 --> 00:20:04.070
the 6 second ads, correct?

00:20:04.070 --> 00:20:07.160
What is the right length
of that ad of the user?

00:20:07.160 --> 00:20:09.210
And that can be personalized.

00:20:09.210 --> 00:20:11.750
So the ad itself
being shown based

00:20:11.750 --> 00:20:14.580
on the content, the
number of ads being shown,

00:20:14.580 --> 00:20:17.620
and the length of the
ads itself, each of those

00:20:17.620 --> 00:20:19.790
we can innovate using
machine learning.

00:20:19.790 --> 00:20:22.690
For instance, you could show
like a brand ad, correct?

00:20:22.690 --> 00:20:24.740
And when shown at
the right moment,

00:20:24.740 --> 00:20:26.840
there's clear data
in the industry that

00:20:26.840 --> 00:20:29.300
shows they will perform
significantly better

00:20:29.300 --> 00:20:30.831
than showing regular ads.

00:20:30.831 --> 00:20:33.080
FRANCESC CAMPOY: So I'm
imagining that for instance we

00:20:33.080 --> 00:20:34.400
have AdSense, right?

00:20:34.400 --> 00:20:36.260
And AdSense, basically
what it does,

00:20:36.260 --> 00:20:38.630
is when you add it to
web page, according

00:20:38.630 --> 00:20:40.250
to the content of
the web page, it

00:20:40.250 --> 00:20:41.952
shows you ads that
are related to it.

00:20:41.952 --> 00:20:43.910
Because if you're reading
that, probably you're

00:20:43.910 --> 00:20:45.125
interested in those things.

00:20:45.125 --> 00:20:46.250
Is that what you're saying?

00:20:46.250 --> 00:20:47.760
That if I'm watching TV--

00:20:47.760 --> 00:20:49.880
I'm watching, I don't
know, like "Modern Family."

00:20:49.880 --> 00:20:52.160
And at some point, I see a car.

00:20:52.160 --> 00:20:54.410
And then they're going to
be like, oh, by the way,

00:20:54.410 --> 00:20:55.730
you can buy this car here.

00:20:55.730 --> 00:20:56.938
AMIT PANDE: Exactly, correct.

00:20:56.938 --> 00:20:58.730
So you might be watching
a Travel Channel.

00:20:58.730 --> 00:21:01.160
And it's talking about
this destination.

00:21:01.160 --> 00:21:03.740
And you see an ad
right at that moment

00:21:03.740 --> 00:21:06.560
from your current destination
to being able to travel

00:21:06.560 --> 00:21:07.430
to this destination.

00:21:07.430 --> 00:21:09.950
There are flight tickets
available for XX price.

00:21:09.950 --> 00:21:13.845
You're likely to convert on that
versus a completely random ad

00:21:13.845 --> 00:21:14.970
being shown at that market.

00:21:14.970 --> 00:21:18.350
MARK MANDEL: Could you also
customize the ad as well,

00:21:18.350 --> 00:21:20.450
like change how the
ad experience is

00:21:20.450 --> 00:21:22.970
for that customer on the fly
and do fun stuff that way, too?

00:21:22.970 --> 00:21:23.678
AMIT PANDE: Yeah.

00:21:23.678 --> 00:21:27.080
That's exactly some of the
very exciting stuff when

00:21:27.080 --> 00:21:29.240
I was talking about
earlier in terms

00:21:29.240 --> 00:21:32.120
of applying machine learning
to the creative process.

00:21:32.120 --> 00:21:35.180
You can think about all this
analysis that we're drawing

00:21:35.180 --> 00:21:38.510
and on the fly being
able to generate

00:21:38.510 --> 00:21:42.270
these dynamic creatives, which
are personalized to the user.

00:21:42.270 --> 00:21:44.750
And in fact, I believe
there's this next wave that

00:21:44.750 --> 00:21:46.580
is going to happen
to ad creatives

00:21:46.580 --> 00:21:49.540
where instead of just creating
a small number of video ad

00:21:49.540 --> 00:21:53.140
creatives that target
100% of the users,

00:21:53.140 --> 00:21:55.340
you will generate a
lot more of those.

00:21:55.340 --> 00:21:58.700
And then with machine learning,
you will be able to slice

00:21:58.700 --> 00:22:01.340
and connect it with the right
users at the right moment.

00:22:01.340 --> 00:22:03.860
And there's interesting
stuff happening in this space

00:22:03.860 --> 00:22:07.580
all the way from being able to
take your base video creative,

00:22:07.580 --> 00:22:10.790
and then overlaying with
context specific personalized

00:22:10.790 --> 00:22:13.610
information, like that the
travel example I gave you

00:22:13.610 --> 00:22:16.550
to products being overlaid,
and then connecting

00:22:16.550 --> 00:22:17.570
those with the user.

00:22:17.570 --> 00:22:19.970
So again, a very exciting space.

00:22:19.970 --> 00:22:21.890
And I think machine
learning is definitely

00:22:21.890 --> 00:22:24.350
going to transform
the way we watch

00:22:24.350 --> 00:22:27.530
ads, and the number of ads,
and the quality of the ads

00:22:27.530 --> 00:22:28.515
that we view.

00:22:28.515 --> 00:22:30.890
FRANCESC CAMPOY: I have a
